        {
          "id": "oldquarter-bach-ma-story",
          "contentId": "oldquarter-bach-ma-story",
          "kind": "story",
          "nodeId": "oldquarter-bach-ma",
          "locale": "en",
          "collectionId": "old-quarter-pilot",
          "datasetRevision": "d75fcc183863f7ea2ec3b24bba1c4664b762c6332f0eb768a3493d9e74089b83",
          "source": "data/localized/old-quarter.en.json",
          "sourceDataSha256": "bec3ff515868acd0462f0b918dff6b4f1bb43d36f98ef063c6da4a44b13730e1",
          "sourceTextSha256": "42afea2a073e26fabb13cae2a0f475cb13a4ee87c2391d2cf4dfb0663f4363be",
          "textHash": "42afea2a073e26fabb13cae2a0f475cb13a4ee87c2391d2cf4dfb0663f4363be",
          "spokenText": "On Hahng Boom Street, number 76 offers another layer of the Old Quarter's memory. Bahk Mah Temple honours Long Doe and is known as the eastern guardian among the four guardian temples of Tahng Long. Its name means White Horse. In the legend retold by the heritage portal, the tracks of a white horse helped a king find a way to build his citadel. Listen to it as a story about divine protection, rather than proof that the event happened. Then turn back to the building. Notice its connected roof sections and timber structure. A place repaired over time need not belong to a single moment, so there is no need to attach every detail to one year. Take a moment to consider how the name, the story and the architecture speak to one another. To begin, you might ask why a white horse appears in the temple's name.",
          "spokenTextSha256": "58546ca363bd83094bc21e7f2f9c749a053d07c3c0c6e1882e9c4e355299b1cb",
          "path": "mobile/assets/voice/post8/old-quarter-pilot/en/oldquarter-bach-ma-story.mp3",
          "audioSha256": "ee55b19e52f87e1259bf1c1723d8568508f9790eee05825e21c1a8ef9b6cbd93",
          "durationSeconds": 61.2,
          "format": {
